1. Problem and Scope
SmartCare Clinic currently uses spreadsheets, paper records, and manual processes to manage patients, practitioners, and appointments. This causes:
- duplicate bookings
- difficulty locating patient records
- inconsistent appointment status
- limited visibility of practitioner availability
- manual cancellations
- unreliable appointment history
- difficulty producing reports
Scope:  
SmartCare v0.2 will deliver a simple, centralised appointment booking system for patient, practitioner, and appointment management. It is not a hospital information system.

2. Stakeholders:
| Stakeholder         | Need                                           | Evidence                                                |
|---------------------|------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|
| Clinic management   | Reliable reports, reduced errors, simple system | Case study mentions operational problems + reporting issues |
| Reception staff     | Easy booking, cancellation, record access       | Duplicate bookings, manual cancellations, lost records |
| Practitioners (GPs) | Clear schedule visibility                       | Limited visibility of availability                     |
| Patients            | Reliable appointments, fewer admin mistakes     | Impact implied by duplicate bookings                   |
| IT/system maintainer| Simple, maintainable, secure system             | Iterative development + small clinic scope             |



3. Functional Requirements:
FR-01: System shall allow creation of new patient records.
FR-02: System shall allow searching and retrieving patient records.
FR-03: System shall allow creation and update of practitioner profiles and availability.
FR-04: System shall allow booking appointments for patients with practitioners.
FR-05: System shall prevent duplicate bookings for the same practitioner/time.
FR-06: System shall display practitioner availability and booked appointments.
FR-07: System shall allow updating appointment status (booked, cancelled, completed).
FR-08: System shall allow cancellation of appointments with reason recorded.
FR-09: System shall maintain appointment history for patients and practitioners.
FR-10: System shall generate basic operational reports.
FR-11: System shall support user roles with access control.
FR-12: System shall allow practitioners to view their own schedule.

4. Non‑Functional Requirements"
NFR-01: System shall have a simple, intuitive user interface.
NFR-02: System shall respond to common operations within 2 seconds.
NFR-03: System shall store data securely and prevent unauthorised access.
NFR-04: System shall be reliable with no data loss during normal operation.
NFR-05: System shall support incremental enhancements in future versions.
NFR-06: System shall support backup and recovery procedures.

5. User Stories:
US-01: As a receptionist, I want to create/update patient records, so that I can manage patient information accurately.
US-02: As a receptionist, I want to book appointments, so that I can schedule consultations efficiently.
US-03: As a receptionist, I want the system to prevent double-booking, so that I avoid appointment conflicts.
US-04: As a practitioner, I want to view my schedule, so that I can plan my consultations.
US-05: As a clinic manager, I want to generate reports, so that I can monitor clinic operations.
US-06: As a clinic manager, I want user roles and access control, so that sensitive data is protected.

6. Acceptance Criteria:
Scenario 1 – Prevent duplicate bookings:
GIVEN a practitioner already has an appointment at a specific date/time
WHEN the receptionist attempts to book another appointment at that same date/time
THEN the system shall display an error and prevent the booking

Scenario 2 – View practitioner schedule:
GIVEN a practitioner is logged in
WHEN they open their schedule view
THEN the system shall display all appointments with patient names, times, and statuses

Scenario 3 – Generate operational report:
GIVEN the manager selects a date range and report type
WHEN they request the report
THEN the system shall generate and display appointment statistics

7. Assumptions and Open Questions:
Assumptions
A-01: Clinic size is small; performance requirements are modest.
A-02: System used mainly by reception staff and practitioners.
A-03: External integrations (SMS/email) are out of scope for v0.2.

Open Questions
Q-01: What exact patient data fields are required?
Q-02: What appointment status categories should be standardised?
Q-03: Are there regulatory compliance requirements?
Q-04: Can practitioners modify their own availability?

